WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.424
Thanks to Brilliant for supporting this episode
of Journey to the Microcosmos.

00:00:04.424 --> 00:00:10.270
Go to Brilliant.org/microcosmos check out
their Scientific Thinking course and get 20%

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:12.752
off an annual premium subscription.

00:00:37.161 --> 00:00:39.581
When you’re in the business 
of hunting for microbes,

00:00:39.581 --> 00:00:42.586
sometimes you have to send 
some weird emails.

00:00:42.586 --> 00:00:49.010
That is why James, our master of microscopes,
sat down one day to send his own strange request

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:54.020
to the people at Coralaxy, a coral farm in
Germany.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:59.719
The job of coral farms like Coralaxy is to
cultivate corals for people to fill their

00:00:59.719 --> 00:01:04.850
aquariums with, protecting natural reefs from
collection in the process.

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:11.700
And while farming might make it sound like they’re
growing plants, corals are actually animals

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:18.980
colonies made up of marine invertebrates that form
spectacular structures painted with vibrant colors.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:23.805
So you can imagine that there are lots of
beautiful things to seek out from coral farms.

00:01:23.805 --> 00:01:28.470
But James was not emailing to ask about corals.

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:33.010
James wanted something a little more unusual
and a little less desirable.

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:38.260
He wanted to know if Coralaxy could check
their filter systems and send him some of

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:41.000
the scum that deposits there.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:47.450
After all, we’ve found fascinating organisms
in fish tanks and in sink drains, why not

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:49.159
combine the two?

00:01:49.159 --> 00:01:56.979
Fortunately, the people at Coralaxy were also
very curious about what their reef tanks might

00:01:56.979 --> 00:02:04.000
reveal when put under the microscope, so, 
of course, they sent James a bunch of stuff.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.690
In addition to corals, which we’ll get
to in a future episode, the generous team

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:13.920
at Coralaxy put together two bags of sediment
and gravel from their tanks, three large bottles

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:19.999
of plankton cultures, some macroalgae, and most
exciting of all to James, a concrete brick.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:24.540
Yes. You heard me correctly.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:28.959
It just so happened that this brick had been
placed in many of their tanks.

00:02:28.959 --> 00:02:33.480
So when James got his hands on it, he was
delighted to find all sorts of interesting

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.190
creatures growing on the brick.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:41.020
Some of the organisms he found were completely
new to him, like this weird living hybrid

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:43.090
of spikes and puff.

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:47.568
Understandably, when James first saw it, he
wasn’t sure what to make of it.

00:02:47.568 --> 00:02:54.250
But then when he zoomed in on those tiny pointy
bits, he could make out the needle-like structures

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:58.367
called spicules that belong to sponges.

00:02:58.367 --> 00:03:03.060
Sponges are sort of like corals in that they are
invertebrates that don’t move around.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:06.530
But they’re also much more simple than corals.

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:11.190
This particular sponge is known either as
a pineapple sponge or a q-tip sponge, both

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.450
of which are excellent names.

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:17.810
They’re found in marine waters all around
the world, but they also often hitchhike their

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:19.800
way into aquariums.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:25.050
James also got the chance to record some ciliates
that he hadn’t recorded before, like the

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:29.404
Peritromus you see here wheeling itself across
the slide.

00:03:29.404 --> 00:03:35.050
And from even closer up, you can see those
individual cilia at work as they push the

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:36.505
organism around in circles.

00:03:39.589 --> 00:03:44.069
One of the fascinating parts of looking at
organisms that have come from aquariums is

00:03:44.069 --> 00:03:51.384
getting to see them make homes out of structures
we don’t usually get to see in our normal pond samples.

00:03:51.384 --> 00:03:58.701
Like these worm-like ciliates called trachelocercids,
which are stretching in and out of a shell they found.

00:03:58.701 --> 00:04:04.140
There are several of them inside there, turning
the shell into its own little trachelocercid commune.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:09.049
Which makes you wonder how they all found
the shell to begin with, and how they all

00:04:09.049 --> 00:04:10.459
decided to gather there.

00:04:13.356 --> 00:04:17.659
And then there’s this ciliate swimming in
the remains of a crustacean’s carapace or

00:04:17.659 --> 00:04:21.430
shell, looking just a teeny bit lost.

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:26.379
The structures in the carapace make for a
harsh landscape under the microscope, like

00:04:26.379 --> 00:04:30.990
columns of icy rocks that the ciliate has
to make its way through.

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:36.779
From other angles, the landscape shifts into
a surreal crystal palace, one that seems to

00:04:36.779 --> 00:04:40.119
have temporarily trapped the ciliate in its
confines.

00:04:40.119 --> 00:04:45.990
But the carapaces that now have formed chiseled
boundaries around the ciliate were once part

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:49.949
of a living, breathing animal, a crustacean.

00:04:49.949 --> 00:04:52.580
So where did that crustacean go?

00:04:54.959 --> 00:04:56.214
Well we don’t know for certain.

00:04:56.214 --> 00:04:59.490
But perhaps they got eaten by this flatworm.

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:04.909
You can see the remains of a crustacean taking
up space, static in the otherwise very wiggly

00:05:04.909 --> 00:05:06.538
body of the flatworm.

00:05:10.349 --> 00:05:14.899
Zoom in closer and you can see the remains
of the crustacean surrounded by a mixture

00:05:14.899 --> 00:05:18.839
of yellow and brown that looks like dijon
mustard.

00:05:18.839 --> 00:05:23.930
Of course, it’s not like the flatworm has
made a tasty mustard crustacean dish for itself.

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:29.839
Those bits of brown are actually endosymbiotic
dinoflagellates that live inside the flatworm

00:05:29.839 --> 00:05:34.069
in exchange for providing nutrients to the
worm.

00:05:34.069 --> 00:05:39.669
This relationship mirrors the mutualism that
corals and algae exhibit as well, with corals

00:05:39.669 --> 00:05:45.539
providing protection and photosynthesis supplies
to the algae, and the algae providing nutrients

00:05:45.539 --> 00:05:48.219
and waste-removal services in return.

00:05:48.219 --> 00:05:54.740
Unfortunately, not all creatures are turning
to algae for a mutually beneficial relationship.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:57.860
Some just like to use algae for food.

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:03.849
Like this ciliate called Chlamydodon, whose
body is filled with its dinoflagellate dinners.

00:06:03.849 --> 00:06:11.110
And inside the sediment samples that James
studied were many gorgeous algae-eating foraminifera.

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:18.000
Those hard exteriors are home to amoebas,
which use their false feet to poke out and grab food.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:23.249
But they’re also an integral part of marine
food webs because animals like fish will come

00:06:23.249 --> 00:06:24.879
along and eat them.

00:06:24.879 --> 00:06:29.550
In addition to the microbial worlds that James
got to explore in these samples, there were

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:34.499
also plenty of creatures who are, by our standards,
on the large side.

00:06:34.499 --> 00:06:37.030
Like this snail spinning around in circles.

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:41.389
We can’t speak to what these snails are
up to, but it’s not surprising to find them

00:06:41.389 --> 00:06:42.389
here.

00:06:42.389 --> 00:06:46.819
Snails eat debris and plant matter, which
makes them useful living vacuum cleaners to

00:06:46.819 --> 00:06:47.999
help clear up aquariums.

00:06:47.999 --> 00:06:51.559
Plus, they’re just cool to watch,
this one has an

00:06:51.559 --> 00:06:56.427
“alien crawling across the empty night sky” 
vibe that makes it hard to look away.

00:07:02.659 --> 00:07:06.709
And in addition to the snails, there were
bristle worms in these samples that we were

00:07:06.709 --> 00:07:12.580
very excited to see, but we’re going to
save that excitement for the next episode.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:17.240
For all the things we know in these samples,
there were also some mysteries as well.

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:21.949
For example, we think this creature might
be a flatworm, cause it kind of looks like a flatworm.

00:07:21.949 --> 00:07:23.740
It certainly moves like a worm.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:29.679
But this is the microcosmos we’re talking
about, a place where appearances are often deceptive.

00:07:29.679 --> 00:07:34.069
And since we can’t identify the species
we’re looking at, we’re not actually sure

00:07:34.069 --> 00:07:35.740
if it is a flatworm.

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:40.169
With higher magnification, you can see these
rod structures inside of it, like a bunch

00:07:40.169 --> 00:07:45.759
of tiny little worms nested within the body
of this maybe-worm.

00:07:45.759 --> 00:07:48.840
Except we have no idea what those structures
are.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:53.669
So if any of you know what this creature is
or what those structures are, please let us

00:07:53.669 --> 00:07:54.812
know in the comments.

00:07:56.770 --> 00:08:02.209
What has stood out to us as we’ve looked
through all of these samples is just how many

00:08:02.209 --> 00:08:05.949
different types of creatures there are.

00:08:05.949 --> 00:08:10.610
Some resemble life we’ve found in other
bodies of water, and some are things we may

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:15.999
not have gotten a chance to see if James had
not taken the step to send a strange email

00:08:15.999 --> 00:08:17.619
to a coral farm.

00:08:17.619 --> 00:08:22.409
It should hardly be surprising given that
corals support such an incredible breadth

00:08:22.409 --> 00:08:23.589
of life in the wild.

00:08:23.589 --> 00:08:29.379
But it is still beautiful to explore that
breadth of life, to see its smallest forms

00:08:29.379 --> 00:08:33.646
thriving because to them, even a farm is wild.

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:44.267
Thank you for coming on this journey with us 
as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

